;;; Syntax probe — equality.
;;;
;;; `=` — the equality *fact*: a reserved head whose arity is pinned at two
;;; ([01_grammar.md § Facts](../../docs/kernel/ir/03-ein-lang/01_grammar.md)),
;;; so `(= a b c)` is a parse error while `(= a b :k 1)` is not. Both argument
;;; shapes are here — an atom pair, and a nested fact on the left — because
;;; that is what the renderer keys on: an arity-2 `=` draws a `doublecircle`
;;; equality-class node with an edge to each side, never an arrow between them.
;;;
;;; It has **no engine semantics in M1**. The loader ingests it as an ordinary
;;; fact and auto-vivifies `=` as an open-world relation; nothing in `ein-infer`
;;; reads it, and the `EqClasses` union-find the store carries is never driven
;;; by one — the seam is deliberate, for a future e-graph promotion
;;; ([02_store.md § 8](../../docs/kernel/ir/02-data-model/02_store.md), F4 Q30).
;;; The *computed* equality is `eq` / `neq`, which are predicates inside a
;;; `:match` and a different thing entirely. `(= ?a ?b)` in a `:match` is an
;;; ordinary relation pattern over that same vivified `=` relation, so it
;;; matches **stored** equality facts and nothing else — `(p x x)` alone does
;;; not satisfy it, `(= x x)` in the KB does. `(eq ?a ?b)` computes.
;;; (`ein-render/src/rules.rs` draws the `eq` predicate with the `=` glyph,
;;; which is presentation and adds to the confusion rather than to the model.)
;;;
;;; One of the eighteen node-kind fixtures. As corpus entries they are
;;; rendered under every parse view and every op, and each rendering is
;;; digested by `corpus_shapes.md5`, so a change to how any of these
;;; shapes is drawn moves a digest. They exist because no *puzzle*
;;; contains these shapes.

(relation r T T)
(= a b)
(= (color House-1) Red)
